<body>
<header><h1>⚙️ Compiler vs Interpreter</h1><p>Two approaches to translating and running programs.</p></header>
<main>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Compiler</th><th>Interpreter</th></tr>
<tr><td>How it works</td><td>Translates a program into another form before execution.</td><td>Executes instructions through an interpreter, often step by step.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Output</td><td>May produce object code or an executable.</td><td>Usually runs code without producing a standalone executable.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Error reporting</td><td>Many errors are reported during compilation.</td><td>Errors may appear as the relevant code is executed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Examples</td><td>C commonly uses a compiler.</td><td>Python commonly uses an interpreter to run bytecode.</td></tr>
</table>
<section><h2>Compiler Workflow</h2><p>Source Code → Compilation → Translated Program → Execution</p></section>
<section><h2>Interpreter Workflow</h2><p>Source Code → Interpreter → Program Execution</p></section>
<section><h2>Remember 💡</h2><p>Modern language implementations can combine compilation and interpretation. The distinction is useful for learning, but real systems can be more complex.</p></section>
</main>
